<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "https://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Mynah GUI (Contents/MacOS/MynahMac) only.

  Nothing else in the bundle gets these. The CLI helper beside it and the
  vendored SAGE.app are signed with the hardened runtime and NO entitlements,
  because neither of them touches the microphone and an entitlement handed to a
  process that cannot use it is just a larger thing to attack.

  Mynah is not sandboxed. That is a deliberate limit on what this file can
  claim: outside the sandbox, network access, file access and spawning helpers
  need no entitlement at all, so the only entries here are the ones the
  *hardened runtime* actually requires.
-->
<dict>
  <!-- Required. Under the hardened runtime a process that opens an audio input
       device is killed on the spot without this, before macOS ever gets as far
       as showing the owner a permission box.

       Present even though Mynah does not record from this Mac today: the owner
       speaks into their phone and the voice note arrives over Signal. It is
       here because entitlements are baked into the signature — adding one to a
       shipped app means a new signed, notarized, stapled build, and the moment
       hold-to-talk lands (Setup/MicrophonePermissionView) the owner would be
       stuck on the old one. The paired sentence the owner actually reads is
       NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in resources/Info.plist. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Required for the calendar switch and the undo button in Settings.

       The daemon has carried this since 1.7.6 (resources/SageVoiced.entitlements
       has the measurements). The window is a separate signature and does not
       inherit it: under the hardened runtime, EventKit is refused before macOS
       shows anybody a prompt, granted comes back false, and the status stays at
       "not determined" forever. Without this key the undo button could only ever
       report that it had nothing to remove.

       Both processes resolve to the same CFBundleIdentifier, local.sage.voicebridge,
       so they share one grant and the owner sees a single "Mynah" row in
       System Settings rather than two. The paired sentence he reads is
       NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription in resources/Info.plist. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation is DELIBERATELY ABSENT.

    It is the obvious thing to reach for once the bundle contains SAGE.app, and
    it is the wrong reach. Library validation governs code loaded *into this
    process* — frameworks, plug-ins, dylibs. It has nothing to say about
    processes we start. SAGE, Ollama and the WhisperKit server are all separate
    processes with their own signatures and their own entitlements; the kernel
    evaluates each on its own. And the vendored SAGE.app is re-signed with our
    identity by scripts/package-app.sh, so it is the same team either way.

    Verified for this codebase: no dlopen, no NSBundle load, no Bundle(path:),
    no third-party framework in Contents/Frameworks. Nothing foreign is loaded
    into MynahMac's address space.

    The trap that makes this worth writing down: a development build is ad-hoc
    signed, and ad-hoc code has no Team ID, so library validation is not
    enforced at all. A missing entitlement of this kind therefore cannot show
    up on the build machine — only on a Developer ID build, on someone else's
    Mac. So if that day comes, the signature is:

      "Library validation failed" or code signature 0xd (SIGKILL, CODESIGNING)
      in the crash report, naming the library that was refused.

    That day came, and this file's prescription was wrong in one detail worth
    recording. The library refused was libsignal_jni_aarch64.dylib, unpacked to
    a temp directory and dlopened by the bundled signal-cli — a SEPARATE
    process. Library validation governs a process's own address space, so
    adding the key here would have weakened every future load in the GUI and
    fixed nothing. It went on signal-cli instead; see
    resources/SignalCLI.entitlements.

    So the rule stands and gains a clause: add the key to the entitlements of
    the process that is actually loading the library, and never pre-emptively.
    Turning it on here still weakens every future load in this process to buy
    nothing.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
